    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> shaped scattered older tales of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> into a founding myth that tied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> and the family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> to its heroes and gods.</li><li>The first six books follow the wanderings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>, and the last six the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>.</li><li>Unfinished at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s death in 19 BCE, it was published on Augustus' order and became the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> education for centuries.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> died with the poem unfinished and wished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> burned; Augustus ordered his executors to disregard the wish and publish it with as few changes as possible. Its many half-complete lines and abrupt ending are read as signs that it was never finished.</p></aside>
    </section>

