    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">6th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Greek vases already show accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> founding a new home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">29 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> begins writing the Aeneid</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">19 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> dies at Brundisium with the poem unfinished; Augustus orders it published</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">4th century</span><span class="rf-text">The grammarian Maurus Servius Honoratus writes the most complete ancient commentary on the poem</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1513</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavin-douglas">Gavin Douglas</a> completes his Eneados, the first full and faithful rendering in an Anglic language</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1688</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-purcell">Henry Purcell</a>'s opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> is performed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1798</span><span class="rf-text">Ivan Kotliarevsky writes Eneida, a mock-heroic burlesque in the Ukrainian language</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> predates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> by centuries. As Greek settlements spread from the sixth century BCE, colonists tied their new homes to their existing myths, and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> suited the purpose. The legend combined Greek, Etruscan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Roman elements: Greek vases of the sixth century BCE already show Aeneas founding a new home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, he was worshipped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavinium">Lavinium</a>, and a fragment usually attributed to Hellanicus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> made him the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> most likely took a full interest in these Greek myths after the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus of Epirus</a> in 280 BCE.</p><p>Virgil wrote in a time of great change, after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and the civil wars ending at Actium, when the new emperor Augustus was promoting peace and a return to traditional Roman values. He took the scattered tales of Aeneas, a figure known since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> but with no fixed character beyond his pietas, and made of them a national epic that tied Rome to Troy, explained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> and presented the Julio-Claudian line as descendants of the founders and gods of Rome and Troy.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Aeneid&#8221; cites where it covers Mythology and origins:</p><ul><li>Celia E. Schultz, A History of the Roman People (Routledge, 2019)</li><li>Jaclyn Neel, Early Rome: Myth and Society (Wiley-Blackwell, 2017)</li><li>Michael Gagarin, The Oxford Encyclopedia of Ancient Greece and Rome (Oxford University Press, 2010)</li><li>Arnaldo Momigliano, Essays in Ancient and Modern Historiography (University of Chicago Press, 1977)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The poem opens with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Arma</a> virumque cano, of arms and the man I sing, and begins in the middle of things with the Trojan fleet driven by a storm that the goddess Juno has raised. Shipwrecked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> tells <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a>, queen of the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, of the wooden horse, the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, the loss of his wife Creusa and his wanderings round the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Dido falls in love with him, but Jupiter sends Mercury to remind him of his duty; when he sails, she kills herself on a pyre and calls for eternal strife between their peoples. After funeral games in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, Aeneas descends with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumaean-sibyl">Cumaean Sibyl</a> into the underworld, where his father Anchises shows him the future of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p>In the second half Aeneas settles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>, where King Latinus is told by oracles to give his daughter Lavinia to a stranger rather than to Turnus of the Rutuli. Juno stirs up war. Aeneas gains allies from Evander, at the site of the future Rome, and a shield made by Vulcan showing Rome's history; Turnus kills Evander's son Pallas. The poem ends when Aeneas, about to spare the defeated Turnus, sees Pallas' belt on him and kills him in rage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> wrote the Aeneid between 29 and 19 BCE, and legend held that he wrote only three lines a day. It is composed in dactylic hexameter, each line of six feet made of dactyls and spondees, with a metre based on the length of syllables. Its twelve books fall into two halves, books 1 to 6 commonly linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> and books 7 to 12 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, reflecting Virgil's ambition to rival Homer, though the correspondence is rough; the narrative can also be read in three groups of four books, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a>, the arrival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the war. Virgil's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> has been praised for its evenness, subtlety and dignity, and he uses alliteration, assonance, metaphor and simile to build tension.</p><p>According to tradition Virgil travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> around 19 BCE to revise the poem, met Augustus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, caught a fever near Megara and died at Brundisium on his way home, wishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> burned. Some legends say he had come to dislike a passage in book 8. Augustus ordered his executors Lucius Varius Rufus and Plotius Tucca to publish it with few changes. The half-complete lines and abrupt ending are generally taken as evidence that it was unfinished.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The poem appears to have been a success from the start. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> is said to have recited books 2, 4 and 6 to Augustus, and the mention of Marcellus in book 6 apparently caused Augustus' sister Octavia to faint. By the 2nd century CE at the latest it had become an essential part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> education, usually memorised, and it stayed central after the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. In Latin-Christian culture it was a canonical text studied with commentaries, the most complete written by the 4th-century grammarian Maurus Servius Honoratus.</p><p>Critics have long debated its tone. Some read it as pessimistic and subversive of the Augustan regime, others as a celebration of the new dynasty; many see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, as a figure for Augustus, its re-founder, and its prophecies and the shield's picture of Actium as support for him. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> of pietas, fate and divine intervention, and Aeneas' final killing of Turnus in furor, have been read as an unfinished Roman ideal or as a comment on the violence of the late Republican civil wars. The exit from the underworld through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> gate of false dreams is among its most disputed passages.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Because the Aeneid was composed and preserved in writing rather than orally, its text shows less variation than other classical epics. Its survival was tied to the schoolroom: from antiquity through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was copied, taught and commented on as a basic text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> learning, and from 1600 to 1900 the Latin school stood at the centre of European education, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> taught at the advanced level. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> it was taught in the fourth year of Latin at least until the 1960s.</p><p>Its unfinished ending led later writers to complete it. The 15th-century poet Maffeo Vegio wrote a Thirteenth Book, widely printed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>; Pier Candido Decembrio began another; and modern supplements include Claudio Salvucci's The Laviniad of 1994 and Ursula K. Le Guin's novel Lavinia of 2008. Translations carried it into the vernaculars: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavin-douglas">Gavin Douglas</a>'s Scots Eneados of 1513, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-dryden">John Dryden</a>'s English version, partial versions by Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey, and Seamus Heaney, and many modern verse translations, among them those of Cecil Day-Lewis, Robert Fitzgerald, Robert Fagles and Sarah Ruden.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Aeneid is central to the Western canon and was long held to be the summit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> literature, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> was of Greek. Its influence has been traced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a>, Layamon's Brut, The Faerie Queene and Milton's Paradise Lost, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/os-lusiadas">Os Lusíadas</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes">Luís de Camões</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> quoted and alluded to it throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> his guide through the Inferno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purgatorio">Purgatorio</a>. Phrases from it entered Latin as Shakespeare's did English, such as Sunt lacrimae rerum, the tears of things.</p><p>It has been retold on the stage and screen: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-purcell">Henry Purcell</a>'s opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> of 1688 with its lament When I am laid in earth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hector-berlioz">Hector Berlioz</a>'s Les Troyens, Italian film and television versions, and Brian Friel's play Translations, which ends with a passage from it. It also invited parody, from Paul Scarron's French travesty, which spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, to Ivan Kotliarevsky's Eneida of 1798, considered the first literary work published wholly in modern Ukrainian and made into an animated film in 1991. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> its Latin lines remain part of the Advanced Placement Latin syllabus.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Aeneid is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> epic of the Trojan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, who flees burning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, loses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a> and wins a home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Written under Augustus and left unfinished in 19 BCE, it was published on the emperor's order and has shaped Latin education and European literature ever since.</em></p>

